{{#if isPassportAuth}}
## Passport authentication

{{#if hasDatabase}}
Set a random `JWT_ACCESS_SECRET` of at least 32 characters. Configure `JWT_ISSUER`, `JWT_AUDIENCE`, and `JWT_KEY_ID` consistently across instances. Apply Prisma migrations before starting.

Register with `POST /api/auth/register` and sign in with `POST /api/auth/login`, using `{ "email": "user@example.com", "password": "a-long-unique-password" }`. Send the returned access token in `Authorization: Bearer <token>` to `GET /api/auth/profile`. Access tokens expire after 15 minutes. Passwords are hashed with bcrypt; token payloads contain no password or role supplied by the client.

Refresh with `POST /api/auth/refresh` and `{ "refreshToken": "..." }`. Refresh tokens rotate on every use and expire seven days after login. Reusing a consumed token revokes the entire login session, including access tokens. Concurrent refresh requests therefore require client-side serialization. `POST /api/auth/logout` takes the same body and revokes that login session. Tokens are returned in JSON; no cookie transport is generated.

Only hashes of refresh tokens are stored. Schedule deletion of expired refresh-token families according to your retention policy. Secret replacement invalidates existing access tokens; overlapping signing keys are not implemented in this baseline.
{{else}}
Configure `OIDC_JWKS_URI`, `OIDC_ISSUER`, and `OIDC_AUDIENCE` for your trusted identity provider. Send its RS256 access token in `Authorization: Bearer <token>` to `GET /api/auth/profile`. The API verifies the signature using the provider's cached, rate-limited JWKS keys and checks the issuer, audience, and expiration. This API does not accept passwords or issue local tokens.
{{/if}}
{{/if}}
{{#if hasGraphql}}
## GraphQL

Send queries to `POST /graphql`. `{ hello }` is always available. With the Todo example, `todos`, `createTodo`, `updateTodo`, and `deleteTodo` operate on PostgreSQL. The first profile is unauthenticated; it does not include subscriptions or federation.
{{/if}}
{{#if hasMongodb}}
## MongoDB

Set `MONGODB_URL` including the database name. The native client connects and pings MongoDB during startup and closes with the Nest application. No Prisma migration commands are required. Todo identifiers are serialized ObjectId strings; malformed IDs return 400 and missing mutation targets return 404. Listing returns at most 100 items in identifier order. This baseline performs single-document writes and does not promise multi-document transactions.
{{/if}}
{{#if docker}}
## Docker Compose

Start the application and its database with `docker compose up --build`. Compose publishes the API on `127.0.0.1:3000` by default (Fastify uses `3001`). If that host port is already in use or reserved, set `APP_PORT` to a free host port before starting; the container's internal port stays unchanged. In PowerShell, for example: `$env:APP_PORT = "3100"; docker compose up --build`.
{{#if hasSqlserver}}
Compose also publishes SQL Server on `127.0.0.1:1433` so Prisma commands run on the host can connect. The API container uses the internal `sqlserver:1433` address. If host port 1433 is occupied, change `SQLSERVER_PORT` in `.env`; keep the API container's internal database port at 1433.
{{/if}}
{{/if}}
